<h2>Storm Exposure Along the I-35 Corridor</h2>
<p>Commercial roofs in Hays County face the same spring hail and thunderstorm wind as homes, often on a larger scale. NOAA records note that on January 13, 2007, <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=12001" target="_blank" rel="noopener">winds estimated at 70 to 80 mph</a> moved north along I-35 in San Marcos and tore metal roofs off several buildings. On May 9, 2024, thunderstorm winds near 70 mph <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1181414" target="_blank" rel="noopener">damaged the roof of a church in San Marcos</a> during the same storm that dropped large hail across the county. Flat and low-slope roofs can hide hail damage under gravel or coatings, and wind damage to edges and flashings can let water in long after the storm.</p>
<p>Hays County is not in the Texas Windstorm Insurance Association area, so San Marcos businesses generally carry wind and hail coverage on their regular commercial policy. That makes the wind/hail deductible and any roof-related endorsements on that policy especially important to understand.</p>

